{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"managing-data-asset-access","skill_description":"Grants, requests, audits and removes access to data assets in an AWS data catalog (the Amazon SageMaker Catalog supported by Amazon DataZone) through catalog subscriptions over published listings. Covers requesting a subscription, approving or rejecting requests with a recorded decision, sharing a listing with a project, revoking one or many subscriptions, and confirming grant settlement. Triggers on: give another team access to a table, request access to a dataset, approve or reject an access request, share a listing with a project, who has access to this table, revoke access, bulk revoke, is the access actually gone, subscription grant status. Not for: IAM roles, policies or STS (use aws-iam), S3 bucket policies (use securing-s3-buckets), Clean Rooms permissions (use aws-cleanrooms), or writing catalog metadata, glossaries and forms, publishing listings, or retiring or deleting an asset that other teams are subscribed to and the order of operations that needs (use managing-data-catalog)\n\n\nServices: datazone\nTasks: deploy, audit\nPersona: data-engineer, developer, architect\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-data-asset-access"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles","skill_description":"Configures EC2 instances to securely call AWS services by creating and attaching IAM roles via instance profiles, eliminating hardcoded credentials. Use when an EC2 instance needs permissions to access AWS services like S3, DynamoDB, SQS, or CloudWatch through temporary credentials.\n\nServices: ec2, iam\nTasks: configure, secure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"amazon-braket","skill_description":"Runs quantum workflows on AWS / Amazon Braket: circuits and algorithms, QPU/device and ARN discovery, LocalSimulator, tasks, program sets, Hybrid Jobs, S3 results, pricing, account setup, and spending limits. Applies to quantum programs and execution, AHS, OpenQASM, pulse control, reservations, notebooks, or Qiskit or PennyLane integrations.\n\nServices: braket\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize, design\nPersona: developer, researcher, scientist\nWorkload: quantum-computing, analog-hamiltonian-simulation, quantum-simulation\n*","skill_name":"amazon-braket"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS Marketplace now enables partners to list once and sell through multiple subsidiary accounts - AWS","context":"AWS Marketplace now allows partners with multiple legal entities to publish a product listing once in their primary account and enable their subsidiaries to sell it through direct private offers. The primary account retains ownership of the listing and grants authorization to each subsidiary to sell specific products and extend offers to particular buyer countries. Each subsidiary transacts under its local tax identity, supports local currencies, and sets disbursement preferences and bank accounts. The AWS Marketplace insights report in the primary account provides a consolidated view of offers, agreements, and renewals across all connected subsidiary accounts. Partners can revoke a subsidiary authorization or connection at any time, which immediately stops that subsidiary from creating new offers. This capability is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where AWS Marketplace is available. To get started, partners can refer to the \"Managing subsidiary account connections\" and \"Creating private offers as a subsidiary account\" documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-marketplace-subsidiary-account-authorization/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS docs on Q Business Q Apps access requirements and permissions."}
